# Sample `rwconfig` file. This file declares the properties that will be
# available to the application - their names, types, allowed values and
# defaults - and optionally configures rwConfig itself.
#
# This one is the full reference and exercises nearly every feature, so it is
# far larger than most. `rwconfig-minimal` next to it is closer to what a real
# application writes. A file containing nothing but property declarations is
# perfectly valid: every property then takes the default declared for it.
#
# The syntax for this file is similar to a java properties file, but extended in
# some areas to support property types and allowed values. See below for 
# details.
# 
# When rwConfig is initialized it will search for this file in the
# following locations in order:
# - the path specified by the command line argument `rw.config.path=<path>`
# - the path specified by the system property `rw.config.path`
# - the path specified by the environment variable `RW_CONFIG_PATH`
# - a file named `rwconfig` in the classpath
# - a file named `rwconfig` in the current working directory

! comment lines can start with a `#` or a `!` character


#
# Library settings. Any property that starts with `rwc.` configures rwConfig
# itself, and uses a syntax similar to a standard
# java properties file. This means that all values in this section are strings.
#

# The config sources to read in order of highest precedence to lowest. Each
# config source is either a standard java properties file, the command line
# arguments, java system properties, environment variables, or a database of
# some sort. rwConfig will read the config sources in the order listed
# here. Any property value defined in a higher precedence source will override
# the value of the same property defined in a lower precedence source.
#
# Information about each of the config sources (including how to load them) is
# declared below this line, using this syntax:
#
# `rwc.<sourceName>.<setting>=<value>`.
 
rwc.sources = args, system, environment, local, organization, app, prefix, \
              directory, json, xml, yaml, db

# All config sources must have a `type` setting that declares the type of the
# config source. The type determines how rwConfig will load the source,
# and which other settings are required.
#
# Current supported config source types are:
# 1. commandLineArguments: load configuration properties from command line
#    arguments passed to the application in the form `my.property=value`
#    - In order for this source to be loaded you need to create the config
#      system with `ConfigFactory.create(args)`. If this is not done and this source
#      type is used in your library settings, an exception will be thrown during
#      initialization.
# 2. systemProperties: load configuration properties from JVM system properties
# 3. environmentVariables: load configuration properties from environment
#    variables
#    - Environment variables will be searched two ways in the following order:
#      - the property name is searched for as-is
#      - dots and camel case letters are replaced with underscores, and then all
#        remaining letters are uppercased
# 4. properties: load configuration properties from a `.properties` file
#    - The setting `rwc.<sourceName>.location` must be declared for this
#     source type, and it must be a valid URL to a Java properties file.
#     - Examples of valid URLs include:
#       - `file:path/to/file.properties` - can be a relative or absolute path
#       - `classpath:my.properties`
#       - `jar:file:/path/to/my.jar!/path/to/my.properties`
#       - `https://my-org.com/app.properties`
#       - `http://my-org.com/app.properties`
# 5. directory: properties from all files in a directory
#    - The setting `rwc.<sourceName>.path` must be declared for this source
#      type, and it must be a valid path to a directory on the filesystem.
#    - The directory is seen as a single config source. The names of any regular
#      files (or symlinks to regular files) inside the directory are used as the
#      property names, and the file contents are used as the property values.
#      For example, if a directory contains a file named `greeting` with the
#      contents `Hello`, and a file named `name` with the contents `World`, then
#      rwConfig will see two properties in the source: `greeting=Hello`
#      and `name=World`.
#      - Leading and trailing whitespace in the file contents is preserved, and
#        the file contents are read as a single string (including newlines).
#      - An exception will be thrown during initialization if any of the files in
#        the directory cannot be read.
# 6. yaml.plugin: load configuration properties from a YAML file
#    - The setting `rwc.<sourceName>.location` must be declared for this
#      source type, and it must be a valid URL to a YAML file.
#      - see the `properties` section above for examples of valid URLs
# 7. json.plugin: load configuration properties from a JSON file
#    - The setting `rwc.<sourceName>.location` must be declared for this
#      source type, and it must be a valid URL to a JSON file.
#      - see the `properties` section above for examples of valid URLs
# 8. xml.plugin: load configuration properties from an XML file
#    - The setting `rwc.<sourceName>.location` must be declared for this
#      source type, and it must be a valid URL to an XML file.
#      - see the `properties` section above for examples of valid URLs
# 9. prefix.plugin: load configuration properties from a properties file, and
#    add a prefix to each property name.
#    - The setting `rwc.<sourceName>.location` must be declared for this
#      source type, and it must be a valid URL to a properties file.
#      - see the `properties` section above for examples of valid URLs
#
# You can also create your own custom config source types by implementing
# the `SimpleConfigSourcePlugin` interface (in the `plugin-api` module). Check
# out the `PrefixPlugin` class in the `plugin-prefix` module for an example of a
# custom config source type.

# Config sources can optionally declare an `ignoreUnknownProperties` setting.
# If this setting is set to `true`, any properties in the config source that
# are not declared in this file will be ignored. If this setting is set to
# `false` (or not declared), any properties in the config source that are not
# declared in this file will cause an exception during initialization. This
# behavior is on by default to help catch typos in property names.
#
# This applies to sources that can be read in full - properties files,
# directories, and anything loaded by a plugin - and to the command line, since
# every argument there was typed deliberately for this application. It does
# *not* apply to `systemProperties` or `environmentVariables`: those are looked
# up one declared property at a time, so the many unrelated entries in them are
# never seen, and the setting has no effect.
#
# Note that the `args` source below is left strict, so running this example with
# an argument of the form `name=value` that no property declares will stop
# startup. Arguments that are not assignments - `--verbose`, `input.txt` - are
# ignored, as are the library's own, such as `rw.config.path=...`.

rwc.args.type = commandLineArguments
rwc.system.type = systemProperties
rwc.environment.type = environmentVariables
rwc.local.type = properties
rwc.local.location = file:app.properties
rwc.organization.type = properties
rwc.organization.location = http://localhost:1520/web-test.properties
rwc.app.type = properties
rwc.app.location = classpath:app.properties
rwc.prefix.type = prefix.plugin
rwc.prefix.mediaType = properties
rwc.prefix.location = file:prefix-test.properties
rwc.directory.type = directory
rwc.directory.path = test-directory-source
rwc.json.type = json.plugin
rwc.json.location = file:test.json
rwc.json.ignoreUnknownProperties = true
rwc.xml.type = xml.plugin
rwc.xml.location = file:pom.xml
rwc.xml.ignoreUnknownProperties = true
rwc.yaml.type = yaml.plugin
rwc.yaml.location = file:test.yaml
rwc.yaml.ignoreUnknownProperties = true
rwc.yaml.resolveMergeKeys = on
rwc.db.type = jdbc.plugin
rwc.db.connectionString = jdbc:h2:mem:test-db
rwc.db.query = SELECT * FROM config_properties
rwc.db.username = admin
# You can use `<<` as a *deferred value* in a library setting, to indicate that
# the *actual* value for this property is located in another config source. This
# is useful for sensitive information like passwords. The config source that
# contains the actual value must have a higher precedence than the one being
# described here.
#
# Even though the value for this property is present in an earlier config
# source, it is still considered to be configuration info and not an application
# property. This means that it will not be available to the application, and you
# do not need to set the `ignoreUnknownProperties` flag to `true`.
#
# In this case, a higher-precedence config source must contain the property
# `rwc.db.password=mySecretPassword` or an exception will be thrown during
# initialization.
rwc.db.password = <<


#
# The next section of this file is used to declare which properties will be
# available to the application, and uses an expanded syntax.
#
# All properties _must_ be defined in this file in order to be available to the
# application. Properties not defined here will not be found by the application
# at runtime, and attempting to set the property in a config source will throw
# an exception during initialization.
# 
# If a property defined here also declares a value, the value is considered to
# be a default value. A default value can be overridden by any of the config
# sources. All config sources are considered to have a higher precedence than
# the values declared here.
#
# At a minimum, you must always declare the name of any property used by the
# application. You may optionally also declare:
# 1. The type for each property (the default type is `string`).
# 2. The allowed values for the property.
# 3. A default value.
#    - if no default value is declared for a property here, the property's value
#      _must_ be defined in at least one of the config sources, or the config
#      system will throw an exception during initialization.
#

# The simplest syntax for declaring a property is:
#
#     propertyName
#
# This will declare a property named `propertyName` of type `string` with no
# value. Properties with no value must be assigned a value in a config source,
# or rwConfig will throw an exception during initialization.
#
# Properties must begin with a letter and can contain letters, numbers, periods,
# and underscores.
#
# To declare a property with a value, use the syntax:
#
#     propertyName = defaultValue
#
# This value can be overridden in any config source so long as the new value is
# of the same type and is allowed (see below).
#
#
# - Value Types -
#
# To declare a property with a type, use the syntax:
#
#     <type> propertyName
#     <type> propertyName = <defaultValue>
#
# The following types are supported:
# 1. string (default if no type is specified): a string value
#    - when setting a string property's value or allowed values (see below for
#      information on allowed values), leading spaces are trimmed but trailing
#      spaces are preserved
#      - in order to include leading spaces, escape the first space with a
#        backslash
#      - that is the only place an escaped space means anything. a space
#        anywhere else in a value is kept as-is, so escaping it does nothing
#        and logs a warning
#    - empty strings are allowed, and can be declared with an empty value or
#      with a value of `\e`
#    - in order to include a literal backslash, escape it with another backslash
# 2. boolean: a boolean value (case insensitive)
#    - values interpreted as `true` are: `true`, `yes`, `on`, and `1`
#    - values interpreted as `false` are: `false`, `no`, `off`, and `0`
# 3. int: an integer value
# 4. long: a long value
# 5. double: a double value
# 6. stringList: a comma-separated list of string values
#    - individual values can contain commas, so long as they are escaped with a
#      backslash
#    - leading spaces are trimmed for each individual value, but trailing spaces
#      are preserved
#      - in order to include leading spaces, escape the first space with a
#        backslash
# 7. booleanList: a comma-separated list of boolean values (case insensitive)
# 8. intList: a comma-separated list of integer values
# 9. longList: a comma-separated list of long values
# 10. doubleList: a comma-separated list of double values
#
# For list types, an empty list is allowed for both default values and values
# defined in config sources. An empty list can be represented by an empty string
# (`intList empty = `).
#
# String lists have additional rules regarding how to handle empty individual
# values:
# - individual values can be any string, including an empty string
# - individual values which are empty strings are allowed, but they must still
#   be an allowed value if the property has a set of allowed values defined
# - in order to include an empty string as a value in the list, you can use
#   additional commas (e.g. stringList secondIsEmpty = `foo,,bar`)  or you can
#   use the escape sequence `\e` (e.g. stringList secondIsEmpty = `foo,\e,bar`)
#   - lists with a leading comma will produce an empty string as the first
#     value, and a trailing comma will produce an empty string as the last
#     value (or you can use `\e`)
#   - An empty string as a value for a stringList property (for example,
#     `stringList empty =`) is interpreted as an empty list--not as a list
#     with one element which is an empty string. If the latter is desired,
#     it must be declared with a value of `\e` (`stringList oneBlankVal = \e`).
#
#
# - Allowed Values -
#
# You can define a set of allowed values for a property. Allowed values are
# declared as a comma-separated list enclosed in square brackets after the type:
#
#     <type>[<allowedValues>] myProperty
#     <type>[<allowedValues>] myProperty = <value from the allowed values list>
#
# Allowed values must be the same type as the property, or an exception will be
# thrown during initialization. Default values declared for a property must also
# be an allowed value, or an exception will be thrown during initialization. For
# list types, each value in the list must be an allowed value, or an exception
# will be thrown during initialization.
#
# Here are some examples of properties with allowed values:
#
#     string[home,work,other] contactType = other
#     int[2, 3, 5, 7] myFavoriteSingleDigitPrime = 3
#     intList[2, 3, 5, 7] myFavoriteSingleDigitPrimes = 3, 7
#
# Any value in the allowed values list can itself be a range of values. A range
# is defined by `<lowerBound>:<upperBound>` and can be used for numeric types
# (including booleans, where it is of limited use) and strings:
#
#     string[0:9, A:F] hexadecimalDigit = 5
#     intList[80, 1024:65535] ports = 1520, 8080
#
# For string types, both allowed values and ranges are case sensitive. Leading
# spaces are trimmed for allowed values and ranges, but trailing spaces are
# preserved. In order to include leading spaces, escape the first space with a
# backslash.
#
# Allowed values support the same escape sequences as values do, plus `\]`,
# `\,` and `\:`, which must be escaped because each one is otherwise meaningful
# inside the list. A `[` needs no escaping once the list is open, and cannot be
# escaped (`\[` is not an escape sequence anywhere in this file):
#
#     string[a\,b, c\:d, e\]f, g[h\]] punctuation = g[h]
#
# Since `\e` is an empty string, it can be used as the lower bound of a string
# range to allow any value up to the upper bound:
#
#     string[\e:z] anythingUpToZ = hello
#
#
# - Examples -
#
# Here are some examples of valid property declarations:
#
#     firstName
#     first-name
#     string myTextProperty
#     emptyString =
#     emptyString = \e
#     string noSpace = no\espace
#     string theValueHasLeadingAndTrailingSpaces = \  all spaces are preserved  
#     long myLargeValue = 5000000000
#     boolean safe_mode = on
#     double pi = 0.314159e1
#     intList luckyNumbers = 7, 77, 777
#     stringList phrases = to be\, or not to be, seize the day
#     int[0:100] testScore = 90
#     intList[80, 1024:65535] ports = 1520, 8080
#     [home,work,other] contactTypes = other
#     stringList notRequired =
#     booleanList[true, false] flags = true, false, yes, no, on, off, 1, 0
#     string backslashes\are\allowed\in\names = yep
#
# Here are some examples of **invalid** property declarations:
#
#     first name = Joe                  // a space is not allowed
#     .rc = /path/to/file               // cannot start with a period
#     1stName = Joe                     // cannot start with a number
#     string[] myTextProperty           // empty allowed values list
#     int foo = 50.5                    // default value is not an int
#     int [a,b,c] myIntProperty = 50    // allowed values must be the same type
#     long[0:100:] testScore = 90       // invalid range (trailing colon)
#     intList[0:10, 100:200] foo = 77   // not an allowed value

appName = MyApp
intList[80,1024:65535] ports = 1520, 8080
string dbPassword
double pi = .314159e1
int foo = 42
string[home,work,other] contactTypes = other
stringList strings = one, two, tree  \,  , four
[one, two, three] foo2 = one
intList [1:10] numbers = 1, 2, 3
stringList firstAndLastItemsAreEmpty = , foo, bar, \e
string no-space = no\espace
stringList emptyList =
intList[1:10] emptyIntList =
string prefix.test
greeting
name
